WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.176
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:08.828
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.128 --> 00:00:15.356
Unit twenty-four: Nationalism. Today you will define political processes formally with 'whereby'.

4
00:00:15.656 --> 00:00:18.215
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.191
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:24.330
Nationalism.

7
00:00:25.317 --> 00:00:29.928
Nationalism means loyalty to one's nation and the belief that it should govern itself.

8
00:00:30.517 --> 00:00:33.793
Nationalism inspired many independence movements.

9
00:00:34.383 --> 00:00:37.291
Your turn. Say: Nationalism.

10
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:41.343
Nationalism.

11
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:42.864
Nation.

12
00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:48.032
Nation means a large group sharing culture, history and often language.

13
00:00:48.617 --> 00:00:50.825
A nation is not the same as a state.

14
00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:53.593
Your turn. Say: Nation.

15
00:00:56.433 --> 00:00:57.283
Nation.

16
00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.225
Identity.

17
00:01:00.217 --> 00:01:03.075
Identity means a shared sense of who a group is.

18
00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:06.275
Language is central to national identity.

19
00:01:06.883 --> 00:01:09.269
Your turn. Say: Identity.

20
00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:13.134
Identity.

21
00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:14.966
Sovereignty.

22
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.006
Sovereignty means the right of a state to govern itself.

23
00:01:19.617 --> 00:01:21.999
Nationalists demanded full sovereignty.

24
00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:25.305
Your turn. Say: Sovereignty.

25
00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:29.249
Sovereignty.

26
00:01:29.983 --> 00:01:30.868
Symbol.

27
00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:35.415
Symbol means an object or image that represents an idea.

28
00:01:36.017 --> 00:01:38.109
Angkor Wat is a national symbol.

29
00:01:38.717 --> 00:01:41.265
Your turn. Say: Symbol.

30
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.182
Symbol.

31
00:01:45.917 --> 00:01:47.162
Independence.

32
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.974
Independence means freedom from foreign rule.

33
00:01:51.583 --> 00:01:54.779
Independence was declared in 1953.

34
00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:58.361
Your turn. Say: Independence.

35
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.480
Independence.

36
00:02:03.217 --> 00:02:04.113
Unity.

37
00:02:05.117 --> 00:02:07.534
Unity means the state of being joined together.

38
00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.266
The leader called for national unity.

39
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.386
Your turn. Say: Unity.

40
00:02:16.233 --> 00:02:17.049
Unity.

41
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:18.633
Movement.

42
00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.880
Movement means a group working together for a shared cause.

43
00:02:23.483 --> 00:02:26.075
The nationalist movement began among students.

44
00:02:26.683 --> 00:02:28.848
Your turn. Say: Movement.

45
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:32.539
Movement.

46
00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:37.170
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:38.017 --> 00:02:41.073
Visal, how would you define nationalism in your essay?

48
00:02:42.183 --> 00:02:49.663
As a process whereby people come to identify with a nation and believe it should govern itself.

49
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.005
How did that process happen in Cambodia?

50
00:02:54.117 --> 00:03:05.927
Partly through newspapers like Nagaravatta in the 1930s, a channel whereby educated Khmers spread ideas about national identity and independence.

51
00:03:07.017 --> 00:03:09.295
Is nationalism always positive?

52
00:03:10.383 --> 00:03:21.081
No. It can unite people against colonial rule, but it can also become a system whereby minorities are excluded or neighbours are treated as enemies.

53
00:03:22.233 --> 00:03:23.896
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:27.435
Grammar focus: Relative clauses with whereby.

55
00:03:27.735 --> 00:03:28.863
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:33.616
Nationalism is a process whereby people identify with a nation.

57
00:03:34.517 --> 00:03:38.246
Newspapers were a channel whereby ideas of independence spread.

58
00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.985
It can become a system whereby minorities are excluded.

59
00:03:42.933 --> 00:03:51.890
'Whereby' (= by which / through which) is a formal relative word used after nouns such as process, system, arrangement or method. It is followed by a full clause.

60
00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:57.206
Be careful! Don't say: a process whereby people identifies

61
00:03:59.167 --> 00:04:02.374
Say: a process whereby people identify

62
00:04:02.674 --> 00:04:04.859
Plural subject: identify.

63
00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:08.354
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:09.167 --> 00:04:10.690
Imagined Communities.

65
00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:15.753
The political scientist Benedict Anderson described nations as 'imagined communities'.

66
00:04:16.767 --> 00:04:22.458
He meant that nationalism is a process whereby millions of strangers come to feel a deep bond with one another.

67
00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:28.682
Print media, schools and shared symbols provide the means whereby this sense of belonging is created.

68
00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:35.393
In colonised societies, nationalism became a powerful force for unity and the struggle for independence.

69
00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:42.173
However, the same mechanism whereby a nation is united can also be used to exclude those defined as outsiders.

70
00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:47.222
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:48.083 --> 00:04:51.023
It is a system blank citizens elect their leaders.

72
00:04:51.323 --> 00:04:55.151
A, whereby. B, whereas. C, wherever.

73
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.653
The answer is A: whereby.

74
00:05:01.817 --> 00:05:03.665
Choose the correct sentence.

75
00:05:03.965 --> 00:05:12.228
A, a process whereby people identifies. B, a process whereby people identify. C, a process whereby identify people.

76
00:05:15.767 --> 00:05:19.380
The answer is B: a process whereby people identify.

77
00:05:20.517 --> 00:05:23.898
An object or image that represents an idea is a blank.

78
00:05:24.198 --> 00:05:27.526
A, movement. B, symbol. C, unity.

79
00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:32.997
The answer is B: symbol.

80
00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.660
The right of a state to govern itself is blank.

81
00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:40.718
A, sovereignty. B, identity. C, nation.

82
00:05:44.267 --> 00:05:46.533
The answer is A: sovereignty.

83
00:05:47.683 --> 00:05:51.134
According to the reading, what provides the means of creating belonging?

84
00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:56.678
A, print media, schools and symbols. B, trade routes. C, armies only.

85
00:06:00.233 --> 00:06:03.498
The answer is A: print media, schools and symbols.

86
00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:08.072
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:08.917 --> 00:06:12.518
How can national identity unite or divide people?

88
00:06:29.317 --> 00:06:30.724
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:31.024 --> 00:07:08.500
National identity can unite people through shared symbols, history and goals, especially during independence struggles. In Cambodia, Angkor Wat and the Khmer language became symbols whereby people from different regions felt part of one nation. However, national identity can also divide, if it is defined in a way that excludes minorities or encourages hostility towards neighbours. I believe an inclusive national identity, based on shared values rather than ethnicity, is the healthiest form.

90
00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.393
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:11.283 --> 00:07:15.956
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and relative clauses with whereby.

92
00:07:16.256 --> 00:07:20.359
Today's value is love of country. Love and take care of Cambodia.

93
00:07:20.659 --> 00:07:23.463
See you in Unit twenty-five. Goodbye!

94
00:07:23.763 --> 00:07:27.239
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

